WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:13.970
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:16.550
In this chapter, we will
talk about neutrinos.

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:19.730
And we'll start the discussion
with a relatively simple

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:21.000
introduction.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.540
How does a neutrino look
in the standard model,

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:26.110
and how does it interact?

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:28.950
We have discussed the
neutrino already quite a bit.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.760
So this is more
or less a summary.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.190
In the standard model,
the neutrino is massless.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.050
It's a massless particle.

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:38.250
And it interacts with
a weak direction.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:41.240
So it interacts with w
bosons and with z bosons.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:43.310
And specifically,
it does not interact

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:47.000
with photons of gluons.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.150
If we look at the Lagrangian or
try to write down the current,

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:53.180
we find that there is a
charged current via the w.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:56.330
And there is a neutral
current, we have the z boson.

00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:59.540
It's quite interesting to
think about those two currents

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:00.960
a little bit more.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.660
So in case of a charged
current, for example,

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:09.800
I have an incoming neutrino,
we can determine the flavor,

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.870
the kind of neutrino we have.

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:15.470
We are detecting the
flavor of the lepton.

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:21.260
So if, for example, identify
an electron in this interaction

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.840
in the interaction,
the initial neutrino

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:25.910
was an electron neutrino.

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:30.110
While for the neutral current,
when we have some sort

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:33.710
of interaction happening,
we cannot identify directly

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:34.700
the neutrino.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:39.110
Hence, we cannot find the
flavor of the neutrino.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:42.025
You can just measure the sum
of all flavors of neutrinos

00:01:42.025 --> 00:01:45.140
in the neutral current.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.510
On that story, neutrinos
have three flavors.

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:53.630
They come in electron flavor,
muon favor, or tau flavor.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:54.830
Neutrinos are left-handed.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:58.110
Anti-neutrinos are right-handed.

00:01:58.110 --> 00:01:59.540
So that's the story.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.610
That's how neutrinos
are characterized

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:03.590
in the standard model.

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:09.199
In the standard model, in
the framework we set up,

00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:12.440
we can calculate cross-sections,
scattering cross-sections.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:17.540
And here, we are looking in
the neutrino nuclei scattering.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:21.680
Again, we can split this up in
the charged current and neutral

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.060
current discussion.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.830
But they go very
much in parallel.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.200
So we have elastic scattering.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:28.820
Or in the case of
the charged current,

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:32.000
we talk about
quasi-elastic scattering.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.560
Then we have an
incoming neutrino,

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:37.280
let's say a muon neutrino,
hitting a neutron,

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:38.960
producing a muon and a proton.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.720
It's called quasi-elastic,
because we do not

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:47.330
break up the target,
but we change its kind.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:51.050
So we change from a neutron
to a proton, in this case.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:52.640
While, for the
elastic scattering,

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.880
the neutron just stays intact.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.370
We can also have nuclear
resonance production,

00:02:58.370 --> 00:02:59.660
where we hits the nuclei.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:04.130
And then inside the nuclei,
we could use a [INAUDIBLE],,

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:06.620
like a neutral or a
charged [? pion. ?]

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:11.330
And also, that's possible in
the neutral current exchange.

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:14.420
And then we have
deep-inelastic scattering,

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:18.860
where we hit a nuclei
or nucleon that hard,

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:21.090
that we'd start breaking it up.

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:23.810
And in this case, we
scatter off the quark,

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:28.220
and we produce a new quark in
the charged current interaction

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:33.180
in the same quark in the
neutral current interaction.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.560
So this is no different from
the stories we had before.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.470
The intriguing part about
studying neutrino scattering

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:44.520
is that we do know that we
have weak interactions being--

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:47.180
we use a dominant force
of being the process.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:51.080
While, if we use
photons to interact,

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:53.330
or we have electrons
being [INAUDIBLE],,

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:58.700
then we can have a mixture
of weak and electromagnetic

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.110
interaction.

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:02.180
Another important
takeaway from this slide

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:03.950
is that, when we
calculate cross-section,

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:06.440
we find that a linear
increase in the cross-section

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:08.390
of the function of the energy.

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:11.870
So while cross-sections
for neutrinos are small,

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:17.360
at higher energy source
cross-sections scale linearly.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:21.635
As we can discuss this for
neutrino scattering with nuclei

00:04:21.635 --> 00:04:25.880
and nucleons, we can also
look at neutrino scattering

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:27.080
with electrons directly.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:29.300
There's a lot of electrons
in the metal around us.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.508
And the neutrino [INAUDIBLE]
with this metal that can

00:04:31.508 --> 00:04:33.170
interact with electrons, too.

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:38.060
And they can live in the charged
current interaction of muon

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:41.600
in an electron neutrino and in
a neutral current interaction

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:45.080
in electron in the [INAUDIBLE].

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:46.970
Also, here, you see
cross-section, total

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:50.780
cross-section scaled
with [INAUDIBLE] energy.

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:52.890
Good.

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:55.710
It's the first introduction
to neutrino physics.

00:04:55.710 --> 00:05:00.200
We'll go into more detail in
the following presentations.

